<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Tecumseh Sherman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1820">1820</a>–<a href="/years/1891">1891</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a> Sherman was a Union general in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>. After serving under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulysses-s-grant">Ulysses S. Grant</a> at Shiloh and Vicksburg, he captured Atlanta in 1864 and led the March to the Sea through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and then through the Carolinas, destroying railways, farms and supplies. He later commanded the US Army from 1869 to 1883.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-tecumseh-sherman.jpg" alt="William Tecumseh Sherman" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathew Benjamin Brady · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gen._William_T._Sherman_-_NARA_-_525970.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It failed in its aim of drawing <b>William Tecumseh Sherman</b> back out of Georgia and lasted until 27 December.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-18">18 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in John Sherman Birthplace, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in New York City, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.71,-82.6&amp;year=1866" aria-label="John Sherman Birthplace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2513 742 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William Tecumseh Sherman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2963 1012h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2963 1012h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.71,-82.6&amp;year=1866">What was here in 1866? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;You cannot qualify war in harsher terms than I will. War is cruelty, and you cannot refine it; and those who brought war into our country deserve all the curses and maledictions a people can pour out.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to the mayor and councilmen of Atlanta, 12 September 1864 (Memoirs, 1875) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4361/pg4361-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I beg to present you as a Christmas-gift the city of Savannah, with one hundred and fifty heavy guns and plenty of ammunition, also about twenty five thousand bales of cotton.&#8221;</p><footer>Telegram to President Lincoln, 22 December 1864 (Memoirs, 1875) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4361/pg4361-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;By attempting to hold the roads, we will lose a thousand men each month, and will gain no result. I can make this march, and make Georgia howl!&#8221;</p><footer>Telegram to General Grant, 9 October 1864 (Memoirs, 1875) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4361/pg4361-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
